    "slot_number": "slot-2-1-3",
    "slot_key": "Temporary role changes during incidents or handovers",
    "slot_value": "During an incident or handover, the person should only act in the role that’s been formally assigned to them for that situation, not just whatever role they happen to usually hold. That change typically needs authorization from control or the responsible supervisor, and the system should reflect it immediately so the radio permissions, addresses, and priorities match the current duty state. Any delay in updating it creates real safety risk, so the change needs to be visible and effective without ambiguity. Usually the change has to take effect as soon as it is properly communicated and acknowledged, with no gap in responsibility during an incident or handover. Until it is confirmed, the person should stay with their usual role and avoid taking actions that belong only to the new role. The only exception is when control gives a very specific temporary instruction that makes the interim arrangement safe and explicit.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-6-1-dyn-6",
    "slot_key": "Authorization and system update for temporary role changes",
    "slot_value": "Role changes during an incident or handover typically need authorization from control or the responsible supervisor, and for urgent or formal contacts, control or the person responsible for the affected area should acknowledge them. Any agreed follow-up should be recorded or at least traceable so there is no dispute later about what was decided. If there is a handover or escalation, it should be recorded so responsibility transfer and acceptance are clear. Seamless continuation is acceptable when it is the same operational purpose, the same responsible party, and the receiving area is already expected to take over without changing the safety context; if the handover crosses a control boundary, changes the accountable controller, or involves a safety-critical instruction or emergency, it should be formally transferred with confirmation. Continuity is fine for the same task and same responsible operational context, even if people move between locations or roles in the background; a fresh call or change in call type is needed when the purpose changes, accountability changes, or the call moves from routine coordination into something safety-critical or incident-related. Usually the change has to take effect as soon as it is properly communicated and acknowledged, because we don’t want any gap in responsibility during an incident or handover.",
